<h1 class="ph">Skills vs plugins vs MCP servers: install commands and pass rates ([[SMX:CD_FMT]] check)</h1>
<p class="answer">As of [[SMX:CD_FMT_FULL]], [[SMX:PASS]] of [[SMX:TOTAL]] sandboxed installs passed across Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, Cursor and Pi ([[SMX:RATE]]% of [[SMX:TESTED]] judgeable installs).[[SMX:BASE_SENTENCE]] Skills (<code>SKILL.md</code> folders), marketplace plugins and MCP servers solve different jobs and show distinct pass rates because they rely on completely different install paths.</p>

<h2>What is the difference between an agent skill, a plugin and an MCP server?</h2>
<p class="answer">An Agent Skill is a portable Markdown instruction folder (<code>SKILL.md</code>), a plugin is an agent-packaged bundle (often shipping skills, hooks and commands together), and an MCP server is a live tool process speaking Model Context Protocol.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Standalone Agent Skills:</b> Installed from GitHub via <code>npx skills add &lt;repo&gt; --skill &lt;name&gt; -g -a &lt;agent&gt;</code> and checked in each agent's skill loader (Claude Code startup list, Codex <code>prompt-input</code>, Grok <code>inspect</code>, Pi RPC <code>get_commands</code>, or Cursor's <code>~/.agents/skills</code> folder).</li>
<li><b>Plugins:</b> Installed from official agent marketplaces (Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build) or as GitHub packages in Pi. Cursor's CLI does not ship a plugin installer, so plugins are tested in four agents.</li>
<li><b>MCP servers:</b> Registered via <code>claude mcp add</code>, <code>~/.cursor/mcp.json</code> or the agent's MCP config, then verified with a live protocol handshake across all five agents.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Which format installs most reliably across agents?</h2>
<p class="answer">Marketplace plugins and reference MCP servers install cleanly at the highest rates, while community npm MCP packages fail more often due to missing credentials or startup flags.</p>
